Subject: ECON: What's wrong with a service economy?

>Subject: Soc: human population
>From: michael.morgan@ehbbs.com (Michael Morgan)
>X-Extropian-Date: Remailed on September 12, 373 P.N.O. [16:44:32 UTC]
>X-Message-Number: #93-9-447
>
>On 09-11-93  01:12, Tony Hamilton - Fes Erg~ wrote to Michael Morgan
>regarding: "Soc: human population arg"... 
>TH-F> We're simply not manufacturing anymore. Instead,
>TH-F> we're servicing, legislating, network marketing, and suing. Oops, I
>TH-F> almost forgot "sulking". We do a lot of that, too.
>
>Generally people are paid more for their services or products if
>these are difficult or dangerous services or products. The trend
>is toward safer and more simple items. Humm. Do we need to do
>more dangerous or risky things?

As manufacturing becomes more and more trivially easy (and when nanotech
comes online, manufacturing, and perhaps even the design of manufacturing
processes, will get _really_ easy), it's only natural for the economy to
shift to "services".  The problem with the term "service economy" is that
it calls up images of hairstylists, manicurists, dog-walkers, and
car-polishers.  Why not instead associate it with architects, film
directors, musicians, city planners, chefs, programmers, scientists,
pilots, doctors, and other valued "service" professions?

If a pocket comb costs $.03 to make and sells for $.10, how many people
does that industry _need_ to employ to meet the world demand for pocket
combs?  Similarly, at the time when the Hexadecium-based desktop brain
costs only $.03 to make (in current dollars) and $.10 to buy, why insist on
maintaining a galley of superfluous assembly-line serfs who might be
_happier_ as fashion designers, educators, or personal trainers? 
(Producing the _design_ for such computers, on the other hand, will still
be in demand, and thus financially rewarding.)

In market economies, consumer demand is the tail that wags the dog.  If the
consumers prefer to buy services, and stubbornly refuse to buy three cars
and four espresso makers per year, then it does no good to bitch about the
lack of demand for manufacturing -- those who want to succeed in business
will either get busy at providing what the customer wants, or go broke. 
Fortunately, demand seems to be infinitely elastic (especially for health
care -- the ultimate growth industry), which leads to my next point.

The other perceived problem with service economies is that naive observers
(like Bob Dole) don't think anything is _happening_ in such economies. 
Dole whines, "What are we going to do, take in each other's laundry?" -- as
if overseas consumers were the only source of wealth.  If the borders of
the U.S. were sealed tomorrow, there would still be a brisk (and growing)
economy here, because demand increases monotonically.  The world economy as
a whole is growing, but because of a rising standard of living worldwide,
not because we're trading with Venusians or something.
