>On 09-11-93  13:35, Edward J Oconnell wrote to Michael Morgan
>regarding: "Extropian exploits"... 
>
>EJO> As far as I understand it, the word "exploit," as a pajoritive anyway,
>EJO> has no real meaning in an anarchocapitalist mindset.
>EJO> Am I correct?

Well, let's dust off the dictionary and see if we can get a real flamewar going:

exploit, v.t. 1. to utilize, esp. for profit; turn to practical account:
"to exploit a business opportunity."  2. to use selfishly for one's own
ends:  "employers who exploit their workers."  3. to advance or further
through exploitation; promote:  "He exploited his new movie through a
series of guest appearances."  [From the _Random House Unabridged
Dictionary of the English Language_, 2d ed.]

You see, "exploit" is only a pejorative if "selfish" is a pejorative:

selfish, adj. 1. devoted to or caring only for oneself; concerned primarily
with one's own interests, benefits, welfare, etc., regardless of others. 
2. characterized by or manifesting concern or care only for oneself: 
"selfish motives." [Ibid.]

Neither of these definitions mentions unfair advantage, trickery, or force
majeure, you'll note; if you can find a different dictionary that does so,
I'd like to hear about it.  So these are only putdowns if you think that
concern for one's own self-interest, in the absence of concern for the
interests of others, is bad.

Note also that the definition of "exploit" does not require that the the
one exploited be harmed; only that the exploiter profit from the
relationship.  So, if a trade of wages for labor takes place, not only does
the employer exploit the workers but the workers in turn exploit the
employer.  An exchange of values takes place and each "turns the
association to practical account" -- has not each "exploited" the other? 
And yet they both seem to be getting what they want.  What, without
government regulation??  This must be stopped! :-)

Since spontaneous orders like biological evolution and economic markets
require a certain degree of self-interest (self-preservation) to function,
I don't think anyone can consistently {claim to be Extropian and denigrate
self-interest}.  And so "exploitation", like "selfishness", takes its place
among the Extropian virtues.  (When I get to seven, I'm going to publish.)

>I don't think so. Let's suppose that through your hard work you
>become a multimillionaire. And let's say that your businesses are in
>sections of town that are closer to the poorer tracks.
>
>I can sell guns, gas and tools to help the discouraged take control
>of their world. Your success in becoming different becomes my
>opportunity to sell those people weapons to use against you. Think
>of Koreans and Blacks in L.A.

Michael... this is so vague that I at first thought you were planning to
arm the poor blacks to help them overthrow the Koreans "exploiting" them. 
It took some serious exegesis on my part to realize that the "exploitation"
you are proposing as consistent with Extropian values is your "exploiting"
them by selling them guns that wouldn't help them achieve anything.  I wish
you had made an effort to be more clear.

I should have realized this earlier.  After all, if you sell guns to some
ignorant rabble, what good does it do them?  They can seize a store, but
they can't keep it.  They can burn it, which avails them nothing.  They can
loot it, and get one storeful of (insured) inventory.  They may even damage
the owner enough to close him down, but what have they gained?  A longer
walk to the grocery store.

I guess I don't see that you're doing anything wrong by selling them these
guns.  The Koreans, if they're smart, will have already invested in steel
shutters, etc., and locked the place up and headed for San Bernardino at
the first sign of trouble.  (Of course, the truly smart ones save up enough
money to open up shop in a nicer place, selling the store to a more recent
immigrant.)  Arms races are all part of evolution; this is just a reprise
of the old "teeth vs. scales" arm race, at a higher level of technology. 
If you're really smart, you'll turn around and sell the steel shutters to
the Koreans, too.  (Sell them through a differently-named front company,
perhaps with a Korean name and Korean salespeople.  Likewise, deal your
AK-47's through the local crack distributorships.)

>Are there ethics in anarchocapitalism?

Well, my own personal aesthetic (remember, I'm the guy who doesn't believe
in objective right and wrong) is for something a little more constructive
-- perhaps setting up a vocational training business in the ghetto to help
the blacks amass their own wealth instead of stealing somebody else's.  (If
I was interested in getting involved at all, which I'm not.)  But I'd never
tell you that gunrunning is "wrong".  Just warn your customers to steer
clear of Extropian businesses -- their countermeasures for breaking and
entering might be packing some significant firepower.
