Date: Mon, 13 Sep 93 13:55:16 PDT
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Extropians Digest

Eric Drexler writes:

>it does not seem that this email discussion is a terribly useful
>medium for making progress on [such scenarios]

I agree progress is slowing, and I will be off the net soon, so I will
try to summarize as well.

I agree with Eric that "A fast military transition [scenario] generating a
temporary monopoly of power appears both plausible and quite challenging."

Whether "fast AIs (or almost-equivalently, Robin's uploads) will emerge
soon enough to play a role in any resolution of this problem" depends of
course on when such a transition happens.  There may be several periods of
temporary monoply power.  I don't yet see how fast AIs or uploads might
fundamentally change the basic problems with monopolies of power.

I agree in principle that "it seems interesting to examine scenarios for
the design and use of automated defenses", though I think any centralized
fine-grain throw-away-the-key system only seems interesting after
technological progress has mostly run out.

I disagree with Eric's summary of my position as:

>Robin's arguments seemingly encourage us to assume that a
>historically-fast, multiple-order-of-magnitude transition in manufacturing
>and military technologies won't have a major disruptive effect on the
>balance of power, and that the associated problems can therefore be
>ignored. 

Instead, my concern is that solutions which would help in the worse case
"large challenge" scenarios of interest to Eric may cause serious problems
in less worse case scenarios.  

Specifically, what if "*today's* voters and politicians, and their
near-term successors" are sold on being terrified of multilateral
competition in the early nanotech era, accept the inevitability of a single
power, and accept the feasibility and desirability of taking this
opportunity to install a centrally-planned fine-grain throw-away-the-key
automated peace-keeping system?  What if the usual dynamics of
international power create a temporarily nearly-dominant power, which would
have otherwise quickly broken up into historically-typical multilateral
competition?

What if widespread fear of nanotech getting into the wrong hands allows
that power to centralize control over and access to nanotech?  What if this
and wide hope for future "nanarchy" helps to entrench that power deeper,
becoming a longer-lasting monopoly power with more centralized influence
over local affairs?  What if a long time then passes, relative to
then-current political time-scales, before technological innovation runs
out, so instead of waiting they build a semi-automated fine-grain police
system with keys held by those in power?  While not what you had in mind,
what if this system not easily distinguished from it by the relevant people
with power at that time?

Perhaps the best defense against central power is to ensure as many folks
as possible think it against their interests.  Any surface-plausible excuse
for central power, such as various "market failures", may be a powerful
rhetorical weapon for central powers, even if the excuse can't hold up to a
closer examination.

The following are responses to specific comments by Eric.  We needn't play
responce-pong forever; it's alright by me if Eric stops here.

The following comments by Eric gave me a much better understanding of his
concerns:

>the rapid, massive deployment of tested weapons systems, especially if
>these closely mimic existing systems save for cost, performance, and
>abundance, or if they are relatively self-contained and automated. ...  a
>technology that can convert a tested prototype into a massively deployed
>weapons system in a matter of weeks. (Yes, there are constraints on the
>weapons system: it must fit existing delivery mechanisms, or be part of a
>self-contained ballistic missile, etc.)

Here is a question to ask a military expert: Is there any one "weapon"
(with a simple design; not an aircraft carrier) with which France could now
take over the world in a year, if only they could make as many as they
wanted from first generation nanofactories?  Eric suggests "consider what a
one month lead would imply in (for example) a newly-started nuclear arms
race", but I'm not sure I see this as a take-over-the-world scenario; isn't
having the weapon-grade fussionable material the limiting factor?

>>Should folks have
>>feared multilateral competition during the industrial revolution?
>
>It did encourage an unpleasant phenomenon called "total war"

If I recall, the causes of this change in war were much more complex than
blaming the industrial revolution.  And many times and places have
practiced total war without any manufacturing revolution.

>>Yet I imagine fast uploads becoming the center of events, holding much of
>>the wealth and almost all the political/military positions,
>
>From the perspective of *today's* voters and politicians, and their
>near-term successors (the ones who will set the initial conditions on the
>game, and the ones I had mind), what you describe amounts to a complete
>seizure of power by unrestrained, unaccountable, nonhuman entities. 

Successors always take power from ancestors.  How are uploads any less
restrained or accountable than ordinary meat children?

>With Robin's uploads in charge, transitions would surely be fast from the
>standard human perspective. Is there a real disagreement here? 

The issue was whether the speed of these transitions makes them more
destabilizing.  As I said, if political/military systems can speedup along
with the technical progress, the usual level of stability (relative to the
spedup timescale) may prevail.

