From: thamilto@pcocd2.intel.com (Tony Hamilton - FES ERG~)
Subject: ECON: What's wrong with a service economy?

Dave points out that doing more service-oriented business as opposed to
manufacturing is OK, since manufacturing should become easier. Rather than
point out, I'd like to _ask_ Dave if he forgot that: This is Okay for jobs,
but not production. In other words, I don't mind at all if service jobs
displace manufacturing jobs, _as long as manufacturing doesn't go away_.
The distinction is that, just computing job market shifts, you still wind
up with the same amount of manufacturing output - it is just that now, robots
are doing the work (or nanofactories, or whatever). What is happening in
the United States, however, is not _just_ a shift from manufacturing jobs
to service, but an actual _loss_ of manufacturing, period. 

>From a global perspecitve, there is nothing wrong with this, but I _believe_
we were discussing the United States. With my extropian hat on, I certainly
don't care what happens to any given country in the long run. With realist
and objectivist hats on, however, I am very much concerned with the current
state of affairs, as it has the potential of effecting me, personally.

So, in summary Dave, I see your comments as being correct, with respect to
the job market, but I fail to see the significance when we're talking about
GNP, export/imports, trade deficits, and the like. US problems in these areas,
I still maintain, are directly affected by a lack of US manufacturing.

