   > Tim, what happens when I put you inside a VR system hooked up to your
   > nervous system? How the hell can you know what reality is then? Don't
   > invoke other people's arguments: quote them. Show me why I'm wrong.
   > Explain to me why it is that such a setup would be completely
   > impossible. 

     I agree totally with Perry, although I do think it can be said that our
   senses come to approximate the current reality over time. [...]

OK, let's take the sense of sight as an example.  I submit that the human
visual apparatus is limited because of the fact that it evolved for the
use of creatures in a particular ecological niche.

The human eye can only perceive a limited portion of the electromagnetic
spectrum called "visible light".  The human eye can only perceive objects
of a very limited range of physical sizes,  which depends on the distance
the object is.  Astronomical objects that are large distances away can only 
be perceived as they were a long,  long time ago.  It is a well-known 
property of the human vision apparatus that there is a "blind spot" that
can be shown by experiment to generated manufactured sensory input.  It
is also well known that people can, under certain circumstances, have their
memory of their sensory data effectively erased due to trauma.  The degree
to which the brain edits sensory data is in some respects unknown.

Our understanding of what reality is is of necessity bound to what we
perceive through our senses,  which evolved for a specific ecological
niche.  We are no different in that respect than cave fish who have no
visual apparatus.  Trying to conceive of what those aspects of reality
would be like a person blind from birth trying to conceive of colors.
Denying the existence of these aspects of reality is like being a
person blind from birth denying the existence of colors.

There are most certainly aspects of reality that we cannot perceive,
and there are most certainly biologically possible "senses" beyond the
ones we posess,  that we did not evolve,  since we had no use for them.
For example,  there could be a "sense" that was related to the sense
of touch,  but had a more sophisticated ability to sense different
chemical properties of substances,  kind of like tasting with your
fingers,  but different than the sense of taste since it would have
a different purpose.  Of course I have no idea what that purpose would
be,  but there is no reason biologically that this could not evolve
under the right circumstances.

There also may be "senses" that some people have and others do not, 
referred to popularly as "ESP".  The problem with this is that there
has been no scientific evidence for its existence,  yet there there is
a large body of anectodal evidence.  Science can only be of use in
questions about phenomena that can be readily reproduced in experiments.
If for some reason a phenomenon is not readily reproduced,  than science
is no more useful than it is in investigating hypotheses that cannot
be falsified,  like whether there is a "God" for example.  BTW,  I am 
personally skeptical of anything called "ESP",  even though I have 
personally had experiences that could be explained as such.  There is 
simply no way to prove this one way or the other,  and any dogmatic 
belief on either side of this issue is an act of faith.  I prefer to 
acknowledge the fact that I don't know (No flying yogis in the house
folks, relax).

So I have to conclude that our senses only approximate that portion
of reality that we have evolved to perceive,  e.g. that which is 
useful for our survival,  and other aspects that we did not need
to percieve as naked apes,  are necessarily unknown.

I can see a future where extropians trade in customized sensory
apparati that are developed for as yet unknown economic niches...

The (N + 1)th Extropian (A)gnostic,

Andy


