Let me at this point declare my admiration for Ralph Whelan, editor of
_Cryonics_ magazine and V.P. of Alcor.

One reason for the large bodies of anecdotal evidence for ESP is that it
sticks out in our memory when an ESP-like event occurs.  If you have a
premonition that Aunt Shirley is about to call you, and moments later, the
phone rings and it's her, the weird coincidence will stand out in your
memory.  It seems so odd that there must be a mechanism.  However, the
thousands of times you were musing about Aunt Shirley and she did _not_
immediately call you are unremarkable, and you don't remember them later
on.  When you look back over your history, all you see are the islands of
"weird coincidence" -- the vast shoals of non-coincidence, when you
visualized something and it _didn't_ happen, are invisible in memory.

Well, Ralph is cataloguing all of those non-events -- he's trying to record
every time he has a "premonition" ("I've got the weird feeling that Dave is
about to call me."), whether the premonitored event takes place or not. 
This will provide some useful statistical data on just how often we have
these kinds of thoughts, and just how seldom they turn out to correspond to
things that actually happen later.  You've got to admire a mind like that.
